<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  KAVACH desktop orb — launchd agent (spec §3 "Background service", §8).

  Keeps the floating orb alive across logout, reboot and crashes, which is the
  whole point of a "presence": it should be there without you starting it.

  A LaunchAgent (not a LaunchDaemon) deliberately — it runs as you, inside your
  GUI session, which is required for anything that draws on screen or uses the
  microphone. A LaunchDaemon runs before login with no window server and could
  not show a window at all.

  Install:    launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID daemon/com.krishna.kavach.overlay.plist
  Remove:     launchctl bootout gui/$UID/com.krishna.kavach.overlay
  Status:     launchctl print gui/$UID/com.krishna.kavach.overlay
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.krishna.kavach.overlay</string>

    <!--
      The CLI, not `open -a KAVACH.app` — and that is the opposite of what the
      rest of this project says, so here is the measurement.

      This project lives under ~/Desktop, which is TCC-protected, and **a
      launchd job has no grant for it**:

          $ launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID <a job that cats one file>
          DESKTOP DENIED
          HOME READABLE

      Nothing here *fails* on that denial. It hangs. Switching this agent to
      `open -W -a KAVACH.app` produced a live process that logged not one line;
      sampled, 100% of its samples sat in a single syscall:

          _PyCodecRegistry_Init → os_listdir → __opendir2 → open$NOCANCEL

      That is Python blocked on the first import in the process, listing a
      Desktop directory on sys.path. The same wall stopped a `next start` agent
      dead in `GetNearestParentPackageJSONType → open`.

      `uv run kavach-overlay` does have the grant on this machine, and the
      camera with it — the log from the agent-started process carries live
      `pinch ENGAGED` and `gesture stop` lines, so hand tracking works from
      here. The bundle's advantage does not apply when launchd is the launcher.

      To run the bundle at login instead, grant KAVACH.app **Full Disk Access**
      (System Settings → Privacy & Security), then swap these arguments for
      /usr/bin/open -W -a __APP__. Without that grant it hangs silently, which
      is a far worse failure than the one it was meant to fix.
    -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>__UV__</string>
        <string>run</string>
        <string>kavach-overlay</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>__BRAIN__</string>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Bring it back if it exits for any reason, but throttle so a crash
         loop cannot spin the CPU. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>10</integer>

    <!--
      NOT overlay.log. The process configures a FileHandler on that path
      itself, so pointing launchd's stdout at it too writes every line twice —
      which is what happened, and it doubled a log that gesture frames had
      already pushed past a megabyte.
    -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>__LOGS__/overlay.agent.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>__LOGS__/overlay.agent.log</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>

    <!-- Nice-to-have rather than required: the orb is a background presence
         and should never compete with the voice loop for CPU. -->
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Interactive</string>
</dict>
</plist>
